-- Q4 [Peptide Bond Formation & Hydrolysis] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Peptide Bond Formation & Hydrolysis', 'A ribosome joins valine and serine to form the dipeptide Val-Ser, releasing one molecule of \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\). For the new amide linkage to form, which atom donates the electron pair that initiates the reaction, and which atom receives that attack?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The serine carbonyl oxygen attacks the valine \\(\\alpha\\)-amino nitrogen"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The valine carbonyl carbon attacks the serine \\(\\alpha\\)-amino nitrogen"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The serine \\(\\alpha\\)-amino nitrogen attacks the serine carbonyl carbon"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The serine \\(\\alpha\\)-amino nitrogen attacks the valine carbonyl carbon"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because in peptide-bond formation the amino group of one residue performs a nucleophilic attack at the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the activated carboxyl group of the other residue. The textbook states that "Amino acids form polymers through a nucleophilic attack by the amino group of an amino acid at the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the carboxyl group of another amino acid" and that "This carbonyl of the modified end serves as an electrophile in the attack of the amine nitrogen, a nucleophile." Because the dipeptide is written N-to-C as Val-Ser, valine is the upstream (N-terminal) residue contributing its activated \(\alpha\)-carboxyl carbonyl carbon, and serine is the downstream residue contributing its \(\alpha\)-amino nitrogen; the serine nitrogen (nucleophile, electron-pair donor) therefore attacks the valine carbonyl carbon (electrophile), releasing one \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\). (Choice A) reverses the roles: oxygen is not the attacking species here, and pairing it with the amino nitrogen as the target inverts both the nucleophile and the electrophile. (Choice B) correctly pairs valine''s carbonyl with serine''s nitrogen but reverses which is nucleophile and which is electrophile—it names the electrophilic carbon as the attacker. (Choice C) keeps the correct nucleophile (the amine nitrogen) but attacks the wrong carbonyl: serine''s own carbonyl is the C-terminal carboxyl, so this would not link valine to serine and contradicts the N-to-C order. (Choice D) is correct: the serine \(\alpha\)-amino nitrogen is the nucleophile and the valine carbonyl carbon is the electrophile. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the peptide-bond mechanism to a specific named sequence, using N-to-C directionality to assign which residue supplies the nucleophile and which supplies the electrophile.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
