-- Q13 [Primary Structure] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Primary Structure', 'A chemist fully denatures a small, single-chain enzyme, disrupting its hydrogen bonds and reducing every disulfide bond so the protein loses all native structure. When the denaturant and reducing agent are removed, the unfolded chain spontaneously refolds in the test tube into its original, fully active three-dimensional shape, with no ribosome or chaperone present. This result most directly indicates that the native fold is specified by which feature of the protein?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The ribosome that translated the chain"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Pre-formed \\(\\alpha\\)-helices acting as templates"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The disulfide bonds between cysteine residues"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The linear amino acid sequence itself"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because a protein that refolds correctly after complete denaturation—without a ribosome, chaperone, or any pre-existing higher-order structure—must carry all of its folding information within the only feature that survived: its linear amino acid sequence. The textbook states that a protein''s primary structure ''dictates the protein''s ultimate three-dimensional structure and function'' and, citing Anfinsen''s Dogma, that ''the fixed 3D structure of these proteins is uniquely encoded in their primary structure (the amino acid sequence)'' and ''is kinetically accessible'' so that ''refolding can be rapid and efficient'' for small proteins. In short, ''the sequence determines the structure, which, in turn, determines the function of proteins.'' (Choice A) The ribosome catalyzes peptide bond formation during synthesis, but the experiment shows refolding occurs with ''no ribosome present,'' so the translation machinery cannot be what specifies the fold. (Choice B) is a reversed relationship: \(\alpha\)-helices are secondary structures that ''arise'' from the sequence as ''regular repeating structures'' formed by backbone hydrogen bonds, so they are a product of folding, not a pre-existing template that directs it—and all such structure was destroyed by denaturation. (Choice C) is a partial truth: disulfide bonds do ''stabilize both tertiary and quaternary structures,'' but they were reduced and then re-formed correctly only because the cysteine positions in the sequence guided refolding, so the bonds reflect the fold rather than specify it. (Choice D) is correct: the only feature retained through denaturation and renaturation is the covalent order of residues, and the textbook identifies that linear sequence as the encoded determinant of the native fold. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply Anfinsen''s principle to an experimental refolding outcome and reason out which surviving feature can account for it.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
