-- Q17 [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)', 'A short segment of a polypeptide folds into an \(\alpha\)-helix. As the helix forms, the backbone carbonyl oxygen of one residue contributes a lone pair to stabilize a hydrogen bond. Within the same continuous strand, this carbonyl oxygen is hydrogen-bonded to the backbone amide hydrogen of which residue?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The immediately adjacent residue"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A residue roughly four positions farther along"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A residue on a neighboring parallel strand"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A residue one helical turn (5.4 angstroms) away"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because in an \(\alpha\)-helix the backbone hydrogen bond forms between the carbonyl O of the ith amino acid and the amide H of the i+4th amino acid, i.e., a residue about four positions farther along the same continuous strand. LibreTexts states the helices "are formed when the carbonyl O of the ith amino acid forms hydrogen bonds to the amide H of the i+4th amino acid (4 amino acids away)" and that these bonds hold the helix together "between amide Hs and carbonyl Os within a single continuous strand." OpenStax corroborates that the hydrogen bonds "form between the oxygen atom in the carbonyl group in one amino acid and another amino acid that is four amino acids farther along the chain." (Choice A) is incorrect: the carbonyl O does NOT bond to the immediately adjacent (i+1) residue; the i, i+4 spacing is what produces the regular 3.6-residue-per-turn coil. (Choice B) is correct: the i to i+4 backbone hydrogen bond between a carbonyl O and an amide H is the defining intrastrand interaction of the \(\alpha\)-helix. (Choice C) is incorrect: hydrogen bonding between adjacent or neighboring strands describes the \(\beta\)-pleated sheet, where backbone amide Hs and carbonyl Os bond across separate strands, not the single-strand \(\alpha\)-helix. (Choice D) is incorrect: 5.4 angstroms is the helix pitch (rise per turn), a distance describing the geometry of the coil, not the residue count that defines the i, i+4 hydrogen-bonding partner. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the defining intrastrand i to i+4 backbone hydrogen-bonding pattern of the \(\alpha\)-helix.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
