-- Q18 [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)', 'A short segment of an enzyme folds into a right-handed \(\alpha\)-helix in which the backbone carbonyl oxygens hydrogen-bond to amide hydrogens roughly four residues ahead. A researcher modeling the helix wants to know where the charged side chain of a glutamate residue within this segment is positioned relative to the helical axis. Where does this side chain most likely lie?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Buried along the central helical axis"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Hydrogen-bonded inward to the backbone"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Projecting outward from the central axis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Forming the pleats above and below"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because in an \(\alpha\)-helix the backbone coils inward into a tight spiral around a central axis, held together by hydrogen bonds between backbone carbonyl oxygens and amide hydrogens about four residues away, while the R-group side chains are oriented away from the backbone and protrude outward from the helix. The textbook states the helix backbone "is wound into a coil (the alpha helix) along a central axis" and that "each amino acid contains an R group side chain oriented away from the backbone," and the cross-reference confirms "the polypeptide''s R groups (the variant groups) protrude out from the \(\alpha\)-helix chain." A charged glutamate side chain therefore points outward, away from the axis, where it can interact with solvent or other groups. (Choice A) is incorrect because the central axis is occupied by the coiled backbone, not the side chains; placing a side chain there reverses the actual inward/outward arrangement. (Choice B) is incorrect because the helix is stabilized by backbone-to-backbone hydrogen bonds (carbonyl O to amide H), not by side chains bonding inward to the backbone, so the side chain is not directed inward. (Choice C) is correct: the backbone coils inward along the axis and the R groups project outward from that axis, exactly as described for the \(\alpha\)-helix. (Choice D) is incorrect because side chains extending above and below pleats describes the \(\beta\)-pleated sheet, a different secondary structure, not the helical geometry of an \(\alpha\)-helix. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the geometry of \(\alpha\)-helix backbone hydrogen bonding to infer the spatial position of a side chain.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
