-- Q19 [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)', 'Alpha-keratin is the fibrous structural protein that gives hair, nails, and the outer skin layer their mechanical strength. Almost the entire polypeptide of alpha-keratin folds into one repeating type of secondary structure. Which secondary structure predominates in alpha-keratin?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The \\(\\alpha\\)-helix"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The \\(\\beta\\)-pleated sheet"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The collagen triple helix"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The random coil"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because alpha-keratin, the structural protein of hair, nails, skin, horns, and claws, is composed almost entirely of \(\alpha\)-helices. The textbook states directly that \"Alpha keratins, the major component of hair, skin, fur, beaks, and fingernails, are almost all alpha helices.\" The \(\alpha\)-helix is a repetitive secondary structure stabilized by backbone hydrogen bonds between the carbonyl oxygen of one residue and the amide hydrogen of a residue roughly four positions farther along the chain, with the side chains pointing outward from the coiled axis. (Choice A) is correct: it is the secondary structure that makes up nearly all of alpha-keratin. (Choice B) is wrong because the \(\beta\)-pleated sheet is the OTHER common secondary structure, formed when extended strands hydrogen-bond side by side; it predominates in proteins such as silk, not in alpha-keratin. (Choice C) is wrong because the triple helix is the signature motif of collagen, a different fibrous protein in which three strands wind together, and each collagen strand is not even an \(\alpha\)-helix. (Choice D) is wrong because a random coil is an irregular, non-repetitive arrangement and is not classified as a secondary structure at all, whereas alpha-keratin''s polypeptide adopts an ordered, repeating helical structure. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall which secondary structure makes up alpha-keratin.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
