-- Q20 [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] medium skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)', 'In a region of a folded protein, two fully extended polypeptide segments lie side by side, and hydrogen bonds form between the backbone carbonyl oxygen of one segment and the backbone amide hydrogen of the adjacent segment. The two segments are oriented so that they run in opposite directions. This region most likely adopts which secondary structure?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A right-handed \\(\\alpha\\)-helix"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A collagen triple helix"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An irregular random coil"}, {"label": "D", "text": "An antiparallel \\(\\beta\\)-pleated sheet"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because a \(\beta\)-pleated sheet forms when adjacent extended (beta) strands hydrogen-bond side by side, with the backbone amide hydrogen of one strand bonding to the backbone carbonyl oxygen of a neighboring strand; when the two strands run in opposite directions the sheet is antiparallel. The source notes that a beta sheet consists of multiple strands held together by hydrogen bonds between backbone amide Hs and carbonyl Os on adjacent strands (inter-strand bonds), and that the pleated segments "align parallel or antiparallel to each other." This is exactly the side-by-side, opposite-direction, inter-strand pattern described in the stem. (Choice A) is incorrect because in an \(\alpha\)-helix the chain is coiled, not fully extended, and its hydrogen bonds are intra-strand, forming within a single continuous strand between a carbonyl O and an amide H roughly four residues farther along the chain — not between two separate side-by-side strands. (Choice B) is incorrect because a collagen triple helix is a specialized fibrous arrangement of three helical chains, not the two-extended-strand, inter-strand hydrogen-bonding pattern of a sheet. (Choice C) is incorrect because a random coil is irregular and is defined by the absence of the repetitive backbone hydrogen-bonding pattern that characterizes secondary structure, whereas the stem describes a regular inter-strand hydrogen-bonding arrangement. (Choice D) is correct: extended strands hydrogen-bonded side by side in opposing directions form an antiparallel \(\beta\)-pleated sheet. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how backbone hydrogen-bonding geometry and strand orientation define the \(\beta\)-pleated sheet relative to other secondary structures.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
