-- Q21 [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)', 'A researcher models a segment of the peptide Ala-Glu-Leu-Lys-Ser folded into an \(\alpha\)-helix and notes a hydrogen bond linking the carbonyl oxygen of the alanine residue to a backbone donor four residues away. The researcher then asks a colleague which features account for the stability of this secondary structure. Consider the following statements:

I. Hydrogen bonds between the charged \(-\text{NH}_3^+\) and \(-\text{COO}^-\) side-chain groups hold the helix in place.
II. Hydrophobic clustering of the leucine and alanine side chains is what stabilizes a \(\beta\)-pleated sheet.
III. The hydrogen bonds form between backbone amide hydrogens and backbone carbonyl oxygens.

Which of the statements is/are accurate?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because only statement III is accurate: secondary structures are stabilized by hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms, specifically the amide H (donor) and the carbonyl O (acceptor) of the polypeptide backbone, and the IUPAC definition of secondary structure holds ''without regard to the conformation of the side chains.'' The textbook states that ''Secondary structures are those repetitive structures involving hydrogen bonds between amide Hs and carbonyl Os in the protein backbone'' and that the helix is held together ''between amide Hs and carbonyl Os within a single continuous strand.'' In the \(\alpha\)-helix specifically, the carbonyl O of the ith amino acid hydrogen-bonds to the amide H of the i+4th amino acid, exactly the i-to-i+4 pattern described in the stem. Side-chain identity is irrelevant to whether a backbone segment adopts secondary structure. (Choice A) Statement I is false: the helix is not held together by side-chain charge interactions; the IUPAC definition explicitly excludes side-chain conformation, and the stabilizing hydrogen bonds are backbone-to-backbone, not between \(-\text{NH}_3^+\) and \(-\text{COO}^-\) side chains. (Choice B) is correct: only statement III matches the textbook, which attributes both helix and sheet stability to backbone amide-H to carbonyl-O hydrogen bonds. (Choice C) is wrong because it pairs the true statement III with the false statement I about side-chain charge interactions. (Choice D) is wrong because statement II misassigns hydrophobic side-chain clustering (a tertiary-structure phenomenon) as the basis of \(\beta\)-sheet stability, when the textbook says the sheet ''is held together by hydrogen bonds between backbone amide Hs and carbonyl O atoms on adjacent strands.'' This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the backbone hydrogen-bonding rule for secondary structure to a specific peptide and distinguish it from side-chain-based interactions that govern higher-order folding.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
