-- Q22 [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] easy skill1 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)', 'A structural biologist examines a globular protein and finds that one of its \(\alpha\)-helices ends abruptly where a single rigid, cyclic imino acid introduces a kink and initiates a turn in the backbone. This interrupting residue is most likely', '[{"label": "A", "text": "leucine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "alanine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "glycine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "proline"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because proline is the rigid, cyclic imino acid whose side chain has cyclized with its backbone amine to form a five-membered ring, locking its conformation and preventing the backbone N-H from participating in the hydrogen bonds that stabilize an \(\alpha\)-helix; the textbook states that ''Proline is consider to be the amino acids that "breaks" the secondary structure of an alpha helix'' and that proline ''is commonly found in regions of the protein where folds or turns occur.'' Because of this, proline acts as a helix-breaker that terminates helices and starts turns (in myoglobin, for example, ''A proline, a helix breaker, terminates four'' of its eight helices), which matches the rigid ring and turn-initiating behavior described in the stem. (Choice A) Leucine is a nonpolar aliphatic residue that is a strong \(\alpha\)-helix former, not a helix-breaker, so it would not introduce a kink. (Choice B) Alanine is the prototypical helix-favoring residue with a small methyl side chain and no conformational constraint, so it stabilizes rather than disrupts a helix. (Choice C) Glycine is also a helix destabilizer, but the textbook attributes its disruptive effect to having ''only a hydrogen as its R-group,'' making it ''too flexible''; it acts through excessive flexibility, not the rigid cyclic ring of an imino acid described in the stem. (Choice D) is correct: proline is the rigid cyclic imino acid that breaks helices and is found at turns and helix starts. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the identity and helix-breaking role of proline in secondary structure.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
