-- Q23 [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)', 'A researcher analyzing a folded protein region observes that each backbone carbonyl oxygen hydrogen-bonds to a backbone amide hydrogen located four residues farther along the same continuous polypeptide strand, with side chains projecting outward from a central axis. This repeating intrastrand hydrogen-bonding pattern is most consistent with which secondary structure?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "An antiparallel \\(\\beta\\)-pleated sheet"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A \\(3_{10}\\) helix"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An \\(\\alpha\\)-helix"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A \\(\\pi\\) helix"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because the \(\alpha\)-helix is defined by a backbone hydrogen bond between the carbonyl O of the ith amino acid and the amide H of the i+4th amino acid (four amino acids away), all formed within a single continuous strand (intrastrand). The textbook states the helix "consists of a single strand, and all the H-bonds are within the strand (or intrastrand)" and that in the \(\alpha\)-helix the R-groups "extend backward and away from the helix axis," matching the outward-projecting side chains described. The i-to-i+4 spacing (4 residues apart) uniquely fixes this as the \(\alpha\)-helix among the helical options. (Choice A) is incorrect because a \(\beta\)-pleated sheet is held together by hydrogen bonds between backbone amide Hs and carbonyl Os on adjacent, separate strands (interstrand), not within one continuous strand four residues apart. (Choice B) is incorrect because the \(3_{10}\) helix is stabilized by a hydrogen bond between the carbonyl O of the ith amino acid and the amide H of the i+3th amino acid (three amino acids away), not four. (Choice C) is correct: the i-to-i+4 intrastrand pattern with outward-pointing side chains is the defining geometry of the \(\alpha\)-helix. (Choice D) is incorrect because the \(\pi\) helix forms a hydrogen bond between the carbonyl O of the ith amino acid and the amide H of the i+5th amino acid (five amino acids away), not four. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining hydrogen-bonding geometry of each helical and sheet secondary structure to a described bonding pattern in order to infer the correct structure.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
