-- Q24 [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)', 'A structural study of a peptide reports two fully extended strands, ...Val–Thr–Ser–Ala... and ...Ala–Ser–Thr–Val..., lying side by side and running in OPPOSITE directions. Every backbone carbonyl O of one strand sits directly opposite, and hydrogen-bonds in a straight line to, an amide \(-\text{N}-\text{H}\) of the neighboring strand. Which secondary structure does this hydrogen-bonding pattern indicate?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A right-handed \\(\\alpha\\)-helix"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A parallel \\(\\beta\\)-pleated sheet"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A \\(\\beta\\)-hairpin turn"}, {"label": "D", "text": "An antiparallel \\(\\beta\\)-pleated sheet"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because two extended strands lying side by side and running in opposite directions, joined by inter-strand backbone hydrogen bonds, is the defining arrangement of a \(\beta\)-pleated sheet, and the opposite-direction (antiparallel) orientation is specifically signaled by the geometry described. LibreTexts states that in the antiparallel sheet the carbonyl O on one strand IS exactly opposite the amide H on the adjacent strand, giving straight hydrogen bonds, whereas in the parallel sheet "the H bonds...are bent significantly" because "the carbonyl O on one strand is not exactly opposite the amide H on the adjacent strand, as it is in the antiparallel sheet." The hydrogen bonds in a \(\beta\)-sheet are inter-strand (between two adjacent strands), not intra-strand. The strands shown run antiparallel and the H-bonds are straight, so this is an antiparallel \(\beta\)-pleated sheet. (Choice A) The \(\alpha\)-helix is a single strand whose hydrogen bonds are intra-strand, formed between the carbonyl O of residue i and the amide H of residue i+4 within one chain, not between two separate strands. (Choice B) A parallel \(\beta\)-sheet does have two strands hydrogen-bonded side by side, but its strands run in the SAME direction and its inter-strand H-bonds are bent (carbonyl O not exactly opposite the amide H), so it does not match opposite-direction strands with straight H-bonds. (Choice C) A \(\beta\)-hairpin is a short reverse turn (about four residues) that connects two antiparallel strands; it is the connecting turn itself, not the extended hydrogen-bonded sheet formed by the strands. (Choice D) is correct: opposite-running extended strands joined by straight inter-strand backbone H-bonds (carbonyl O exactly opposite amide H) define the antiparallel \(\beta\)-pleated sheet. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural criteria for secondary structures to an unlabeled hydrogen-bonding pattern and infer the specific fold it represents.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
