-- Q25 [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] hard skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Amino Acids, Peptides, and Proteins', 'Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)', 'A biochemist characterizes a short segment of a folded polypeptide and records several structural observations about it. Considering which features define a protein''s secondary structure, which of the following observations describe that level of organization?

I. The segment''s conformation is held together by hydrogen bonds between backbone amide hydrogens and carbonyl oxygens.
II. The segment is stabilized chiefly by noncovalent contacts between side chains that are far apart in the linear sequence.
III. The backbone adopts a repetitive, regular conformation such as an \(\alpha\)-helix or \(\beta\)-pleated sheet.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and II only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because secondary structure is defined as the repetitive conformational arrangement of the backbone segments, held together by hydrogen bonds between the peptide backbone amide hydrogens and carbonyl oxygens, and "without regard to the conformation of the side chains." Statement I names exactly this backbone amide-H/carbonyl-O hydrogen bonding criterion, and statement III names the regular repetitive backbone conformations (the \(\alpha\)-helix and \(\beta\)-pleated sheet) the IUPAC definition lists; both therefore describe secondary structure. Statement II describes noncovalent contacts between distant side chains, which is the protein''s overall 3D (tertiary) structure, not secondary structure. (Choice A) is correct: I (backbone amide-H to carbonyl-O hydrogen bonding) and III (repetitive \(\alpha\)-helix/\(\beta\)-sheet backbone conformation) are precisely the two defining features of secondary structure, while side-chain-based statement II is excluded. (Choice B) is incorrect because it drops statement I; the backbone amide-H/carbonyl-O hydrogen bonding in I is itself a defining feature of secondary structure, so III alone is incomplete. (Choice C) is incorrect because it includes statement II, which describes side-chain noncovalent contacts (tertiary structure), and the secondary-structure definition applies "without regard to the conformation of the side chains." (Choice D) is incorrect because it pairs the tertiary-level statement II with III while omitting the H-bonding criterion in I, reversing which observations belong to secondary versus tertiary structure. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the defining criteria of secondary structure (local, repetitive, backbone-hydrogen-bonded) and distinguish them from features of tertiary structure.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
